1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,542
In week one, Aarav puts
fifty rupees in a jar.

2
00:00:02,942 --> 00:00:05,754
Every week after that, he
adds twenty rupees more.

3
00:00:05,754 --> 00:00:07,795
The same amount, every single week.

4
00:00:08,245 --> 00:00:12,327
He wants to know how much will be
in the jar after one whole year.

5
00:00:12,327 --> 00:00:13,932
That is fifty two weeks.

6
00:00:14,282 --> 00:00:18,548
Does he have to add twenty, fifty
one times, one week at a time?

7
00:00:19,048 --> 00:00:19,948
No.

8
00:00:19,498 --> 00:00:22,777
This pattern has a name,
and a one line formula.

9
00:00:22,777 --> 00:00:25,283
It is called an arithmetic progression.

10
00:00:25,933 --> 00:00:27,667
Look at the jar each week.

11
00:00:27,667 --> 00:00:30,308
Fifty, seventy, ninety, one hundred ten.

12
00:00:30,708 --> 00:00:34,382
Each total is the one before
it, plus the same twenty.

13
00:00:34,382 --> 00:00:35,808
Twenty is the step.

14
00:00:36,208 --> 00:00:40,759
A sequence like this is an arithmetic
progression, or AP for short.

15
00:00:40,759 --> 00:00:44,435
Each term is the term before
it, plus a fixed number.

16
00:00:45,085 --> 00:00:46,966
The first term is called a.

17
00:00:46,966 --> 00:00:50,220
The fixed step is called
d, the common difference.

18
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:52,600
Here, a is fifty and d is twenty.

19
00:00:53,250 --> 00:00:55,061
Now watch the weeks go by.

20
00:00:55,061 --> 00:00:58,341
n counts up, and each new
bar is one step taller.

21
00:00:58,741 --> 00:01:01,059
Week two is fifty plus one step.

22
00:01:01,059 --> 00:01:03,530
Week three is fifty plus two steps.

23
00:01:03,530 --> 00:01:05,991
Week four, fifty plus three steps.

24
00:01:06,391 --> 00:01:07,409
See the pattern?

25
00:01:07,409 --> 00:01:10,698
The number of steps is always
one less than the week number.

26
00:01:10,698 --> 00:01:13,217
Join the tops, and you
get a straight line.

27
00:01:13,867 --> 00:01:16,720
Now let us build the
formula in small steps.

28
00:01:17,070 --> 00:01:17,970
Step one.

29
00:01:17,830 --> 00:01:19,266
The first term is a.

30
00:01:19,266 --> 00:01:20,267
No steps yet.

31
00:01:21,017 --> 00:01:21,917
Step two.

32
00:01:21,772 --> 00:01:23,676
The second term is a plus d.

33
00:01:23,676 --> 00:01:25,488
The third is a plus two d.

34
00:01:26,238 --> 00:01:27,175
Step three.

35
00:01:27,175 --> 00:01:30,381
To reach term n, you
take n minus one steps.

36
00:01:30,381 --> 00:01:32,855
So you add d, n minus one times.

37
00:01:33,605 --> 00:01:38,050
So the nth term is a n equals
a, plus n minus one, times d.

38
00:01:38,050 --> 00:01:40,676
That is the general term of an AP.

39
00:01:41,326 --> 00:01:42,367
Side by side.

40
00:01:42,367 --> 00:01:46,129
When d is positive, the terms
climb, like Aarav's savings.

41
00:01:46,129 --> 00:01:47,846
Fifty, seventy, ninety.

42
00:01:48,246 --> 00:01:50,478
When d is negative, the terms fall.

43
00:01:50,478 --> 00:01:54,978
Spending ten rupees a day from one
hundred gives one hundred, ninety, eighty.

44
00:01:54,978 --> 00:01:56,350
Here d is minus ten.

45
00:01:56,950 --> 00:01:57,912
Now you try.

46
00:01:57,912 --> 00:02:01,702
In ten, seven, four, one,
is d three, or minus three?

47
00:02:01,702 --> 00:02:02,897
Pause and decide.

48
00:02:06,097 --> 00:02:07,074
Minus three!

49
00:02:07,074 --> 00:02:10,146
d is always a term minus
the term before it.

50
00:02:10,146 --> 00:02:12,321
Seven minus ten is minus three.

51
00:02:12,971 --> 00:02:15,862
How do you check if a sequence is an AP?

52
00:02:15,862 --> 00:02:18,324
Subtract each term from the next one.

53
00:02:18,324 --> 00:02:21,558
If every difference is
the same, it is an AP.

54
00:02:21,958 --> 00:02:22,858
Quick one.

55
00:02:22,773 --> 00:02:25,761
Is three, six, twelve, twenty four an AP?

56
00:02:25,761 --> 00:02:26,913
Pause and think.

57
00:02:30,113 --> 00:02:31,013
No!

58
00:02:30,497 --> 00:02:32,897
The differences are three, six and twelve.

59
00:02:32,897 --> 00:02:35,589
They are not the same, so it is not an AP.

60
00:02:36,239 --> 00:02:37,408
Remember this.

61
00:02:37,408 --> 00:02:40,467
In an AP, add the same d every time.

62
00:02:40,467 --> 00:02:43,974
The nth term is a plus
n minus one, times d.

63
00:02:44,674 --> 00:02:45,867
Now, Aarav's question.

64
00:02:45,867 --> 00:02:47,499
How much after fifty two weeks?

65
00:02:47,849 --> 00:02:48,749
Step one.

66
00:02:48,689 --> 00:02:52,083
a is fifty, d is twenty,
and n is fifty two.

67
00:02:52,833 --> 00:02:53,733
Step two.

68
00:02:53,684 --> 00:02:57,468
a fifty two equals fifty,
plus fifty one times twenty.

69
00:02:58,218 --> 00:02:59,118
Step three.

70
00:02:59,111 --> 00:03:02,303
Fifty one times twenty is
one thousand and twenty.

71
00:03:03,053 --> 00:03:07,172
So after one year, the jar holds
fifty plus one thousand and twenty.

72
00:03:07,172 --> 00:03:09,599
That is one thousand and seventy rupees.

73
00:03:10,249 --> 00:03:11,713
Now, the exam trap.

74
00:03:12,063 --> 00:03:14,766
Find the tenth term of
five, eight, eleven.

75
00:03:14,766 --> 00:03:18,645
Many students write five plus ten
times three, and get thirty five.

76
00:03:19,045 --> 00:03:22,565
But the tenth term takes only
nine steps from the first.

77
00:03:22,565 --> 00:03:26,285
So it is five plus nine times
three, which is thirty two.

78
00:03:26,935 --> 00:03:28,291
Where is this asked?

79
00:03:28,291 --> 00:03:32,313
Arithmetic progressions are taught
in the NCERT Class ten book, and are

80
00:03:32,313 --> 00:03:34,606
not in the current Class eleven text.

81
00:03:34,606 --> 00:03:38,510
JEE Main asks them, for example
with terms and sums of an AP.

82
00:03:39,110 --> 00:03:40,254
Exam level now.

83
00:03:40,254 --> 00:03:42,862
Which term of this AP is ninety nine?

84
00:03:42,862 --> 00:03:44,055
Pause and solve.

85
00:03:49,455 --> 00:03:50,706
The answer is A.

86
00:03:50,706 --> 00:03:52,296
The twenty fourth term.

87
00:03:52,796 --> 00:03:56,330
From the fifth term to the
twelfth term is seven steps.

88
00:03:56,330 --> 00:03:59,206
Fifty one minus twenty
three is twenty eight.

89
00:03:59,206 --> 00:04:02,151
So seven d is twenty eight, and d is four.

90
00:04:02,651 --> 00:04:04,981
The fifth term is a plus four d.

91
00:04:04,981 --> 00:04:08,482
So a is twenty three minus
sixteen, which is seven.

92
00:04:08,982 --> 00:04:13,419
Now seven plus n minus one,
times four, equals ninety nine.

93
00:04:13,419 --> 00:04:17,484
So n minus one is twenty
three, and n is twenty four.

94
00:04:18,084 --> 00:04:19,624
Here is your recap.

95
00:04:19,624 --> 00:04:21,207
Screenshot this card.

96
00:04:24,407 --> 00:04:26,588
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

97
00:04:26,988 --> 00:04:27,888
One.

98
00:04:27,482 --> 00:04:31,063
What is the common difference
of twenty, fifteen, ten?

99
00:04:31,963 --> 00:04:32,863
Two.

100
00:04:32,439 --> 00:04:35,360
Find the fifteenth term
of two, five, eight.

101
00:04:36,260 --> 00:04:37,160
Three.

102
00:04:36,862 --> 00:04:39,607
Is one, four, nine, sixteen an AP?

103
00:04:39,607 --> 00:04:40,507
Why?

104
00:04:41,210 --> 00:04:45,586
Next time: can you add all the numbers
from one to one hundred in seconds?

105
00:04:45,586 --> 00:04:46,673
Young Gauss did.

106
00:04:46,673 --> 00:04:48,971
We find out with the sum of an AP.
